    pr_ref:  # b21b
    description: "FOUND 2026-10-01 by WI-gokop-nudub-zunal-zibij-fuvad-libot-bituk-visid (branch jgstern-agent/feat/gokop-scala-inferred-receivers, not yet integrated).\n\nWHAT. scala.py does not expand a type alias. lila declares `type Fu[A] = Future[A]` (and `Funit`), and writes its async API in those names. With WI-gokop's return-type registry, a receiver bound to such a call is now typed -- but typed `Fu` / `Funit`, a name that qualifies to no module: on lila, 951 newly-gained `receiver_type_hint`s are `Fu` and 65 are `Funit`, and every one of them keeps the `external` module slot. The Future's own methods (`map`, `flatMap`, `dmap` via extension) therefore reach no catalogue row and the element type is never read (an alias is not in _SCALA_ELEMENT_CONTAINERS, so `fu.map(x => x.foo())` leaves `x` untyped).\n\nMEASURED (A/B, same core, origin/dev vs the WI-gokop branch): lila 951 + 65 hints, slot external. sbt: not measured for aliases.\n\nFIX SHAPE (not designed): record `type_definition` aliases in Pass 1 (name -> right-hand type node, with its type parameters), expand an alias when naming a type (_type_node_name) and when reading an element (_generic_element), first writer wins like the other registries. Precision: an alias is name-keyed like everything else here; same-named aliases in different packages collide."  # b21b
